{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Research Translation for Learning Design","title":"Ethical Reporting of AI Use","description":"AIL 606 textbook section connecting ethical reporting of ai use to AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Distinguish a vague disclosure (\"AI was used\") from a bounded one that names the decision, the AI contribution, and the verification.","Reject accountability-shedding language (the output is \"objective\" because AI made it) and keep responsibility with the human.","Document accept/reject calls on AI suggestions, with the reason for each.","Decide the right granularity of disclosure: enough for a reviewer to judge provenance, not a full transcript."],"concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"course":"ail606-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"07","prereq_section_ids":["ail606-supplement/07/06"],"practice_ids":["ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_artifact_signal","ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_annotation_signal","ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_ai_boundary","ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_calibration"]},"content":"# Ethical Reporting of AI Use\n\n![Ethical Reporting of AI Use textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/ail606-supplement/07-07-ethical-reporting-of-ai-use.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen a learning designer uses a generative model to draft an item, summarize a source, or propose a layout, the question is not whether to disclose but how to disclose well. A good disclosure does three things at once: it scopes the AI's contribution to specific, bounded decisions; it makes the human verification visible; and it keeps accountability with the person, not the tool. The failure modes are predictable: a vague \"AI was used\" that names nothing checkable, and an accountability-shedding claim that the output is \"objective\" because a machine produced it. This section teaches the bounded, honest disclosure that publication venues, granting agencies, and course policies increasingly require.\n\nThis matters because disclosure is where intellectual honesty becomes operational. A reviewer cannot judge the provenance of your work from a slogan; they need to know which decision the AI touched and how you checked it. A learning designer who can write that disclosure precisely is demonstrating the same judgment the rest of the chapter teaches: evidence over assertion, accountability over hand-waving.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish a vague disclosure (\"AI was used\") from a bounded one that names the decision, the AI contribution, and the verification.\n- Reject accountability-shedding language (the output is \"objective\" because AI made it) and keep responsibility with the human.\n- Document accept/reject calls on AI suggestions, with the reason for each.\n- Decide the right granularity of disclosure: enough for a reviewer to judge provenance, not a full transcript.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA draft report ends with: \"AI was used in this project. All analysis was AI-generated and is therefore objective.\" Two sentences, two failures. \"Was used\" names nothing a reviewer could verify, so it discloses in form but not in substance. \"Therefore objective\" tries to launder accountability through the tool, as if a model's involvement could relieve the designer of responsibility for choices the designer in fact made. The disclosure reads as a shield rather than a record. This section replaces it with a statement a reviewer can actually use to judge provenance.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn ethical AI-use disclosure is built from four elements, each closing a specific gap:\n\n- **Scope:** which bounded decision the AI supported (e.g., drafting alternative item stems for screen 4), not a blanket \"the project.\"\n- **Contribution:** what the AI actually produced, stated concretely enough that a reader can picture it (three candidate stems, a summary, a layout option).\n- **Accept/reject calls:** which suggestions you kept, which you changed, which you discarded, and the reason for each, because the editorial judgment is yours and is the point of interest.\n- **Verification and accountability:** how you checked the output and an explicit statement that you remain responsible for the result. Accountability does not transfer to a tool.\n\nThe unifying principle is that disclosure exists so others can judge provenance, which means it must be specific and honest rather than reassuring. A disclosure that makes the work sound more authoritative (\"objective,\" \"validated by AI\") is doing the opposite of its job.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"Scope\",\"definition\":\"Names the bounded decision the AI supported, such as drafting alternative item stems for screen 4, not a blanket the project.\"},{\"term\":\"Contribution\",\"definition\":\"States concretely what the AI produced, such as three candidate stems, so a reader can picture it.\"},{\"term\":\"Accept/reject calls\",\"definition\":\"Records which suggestions you kept, changed, or discarded and why, because the editorial judgment is yours and is the point of interest.\"},{\"term\":\"Verification and accountability\",\"definition\":\"States how you checked the output and that you remain responsible; accountability does not transfer to the tool.\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"A report states: 'All analysis was AI-generated and is therefore objective.' Which disclosure failure is this?\" explanation=\"The phrase tries to launder accountability through the tool, as if a model's involvement could relieve the designer of responsibility for choices the designer made. It is an accountability-shedding claim, not an honest record.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It is a strong disclosure because it credits the AI.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It sheds accountability by claiming the tool makes the work objective.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is fine in scope but should add a confidence score.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe course anchors inform even this. **Mayer's coherence principle** argues for a disclosure that is complete but not bloated; a full chat log adds extraneous material that obscures the decisions a reviewer needs. **Cognitive load theory** supports a structured disclosure (decision, contribution, calls, verification) that a reader can parse quickly rather than a narrative they must mine. **UDL learner variability** is relevant when AI assists with content for diverse learners: a disclosure should note where you checked that AI-drafted material did not encode bias or assume a single learner profile, since that is a verification a reviewer will rightly want.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nTrace one AI-use disclosure from a vague, accountability-shedding line to an honest, bounded statement.\n\n**Before (disclosure excerpt, first draft):**\n\n> AI was used in this project. All analysis was AI-generated and is therefore objective.\n\n1. \"Was used\" names nothing checkable, so it discloses in form only.\n2. \"Therefore objective\" tries to hand accountability to the tool, which a designer cannot do.\n3. There is no scope, no contribution detail, and no verification.\n\n**After (bounded disclosure excerpt):**\n\n> AI-use note: To revise the screen-4 item stems, I prompted a generative model for three alternative stems. I accepted two after checking them against the rubric, rewrote one for reading level, and discarded one that tested a construct outside the lesson. I verified all final wording and remain responsible for the items.\n\nThe revised version is honest and bounded: it names the decision (screen-4 stems), what the AI produced (three stems), the accept/reject/rewrite calls with reasons, and the human verification. Accountability stays with the designer.\n\nOne rejected claim: that the output was \"objective.\" I removed it because the items reflect choices I made (which stems to keep, how to rewrite for reading level, which construct to exclude) and I must answer for them. The disclosure now lets a reviewer judge provenance instead of trusting a slogan.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore any AI-use statement ships, test it against four questions:\n\n1. **Scope check:** Does it name the specific bounded decision the AI touched, rather than \"the project\"? Vague scope means no real disclosure.\n2. **Contribution check:** Could a reader picture what the AI actually produced? If not, add the concrete detail.\n3. **Calls check:** Does it record what you accepted, changed, and rejected, with reasons? The editorial judgment is the part worth disclosing.\n4. **Accountability check:** Does the statement keep responsibility with you, with no claim that the tool's involvement confers objectivity or relieves you of judgment?\n\nA disclosure that fails the accountability check is not merely incomplete; it misrepresents who is responsible, which is an ethical fault, not a stylistic one.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Rewrite a weak AI-use disclosure\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Your draft ends: \\\"AI was used in this project. All analysis was AI-generated and is therefore objective.\\\" A reviewer flags it. What is your first repair?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Add \\\"the model is state-of-the-art\\\" to sound more rigorous.\",\"next\":\"credential\",\"feedback\":\"That inverts the purpose; disclosure is for scrutiny, not endorsement.\"},{\"label\":\"Run the scope check: name the specific bounded decision the AI touched.\",\"next\":\"scope\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Vague scope means no real disclosure.\"}]},\"credential\":{\"prompt\":\"A reviewer reads the endorsement as a red flag and trusts the work less. Back up: what does honest disclosure require first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Name the bounded decision the AI actually touched.\",\"next\":\"scope\",\"feedback\":\"Yes, scrutiny over salesmanship.\"}]},\"scope\":{\"prompt\":\"You scope it: the AI drafted three alternative item stems for screen 4. Now the accountability-shedding clause. What do you do with \\\"therefore objective\\\"?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep it; the AI did the drafting, so it is neutral.\",\"next\":\"keep\",\"feedback\":\"The items reflect choices you made, so you must answer for them; objectivity cannot be claimed.\"},{\"label\":\"Remove it and state you remain responsible for the items.\",\"next\":\"accountable\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Accountability stays with the human.\"}]},\"keep\":{\"prompt\":\"\\\"Objective\\\" still hands responsibility to the tool for choices you made (which stems to keep, how to rewrite). What is the fix?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Delete the objectivity claim and assert your responsibility.\",\"next\":\"accountable\",\"feedback\":\"Yes.\"}]},\"accountable\":{\"prompt\":\"You add the accept/reject calls: accepted two stems after checking the rubric, rewrote one for reading level, discarded one testing a construct outside the lesson, verified all final wording. Is the disclosure now usable?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Yes; a reviewer can now judge provenance instead of trusting a slogan.\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Scope, contribution, calls, verification, and accountability are all present.\"},{\"label\":\"No; you should attach the full chat transcript.\",\"next\":\"transcript\",\"feedback\":\"A full log is extraneous; the structured account is the signal a reviewer needs.\"}]},\"transcript\":{\"prompt\":\"A full transcript buries the decisions a reviewer needs. What granularity is right?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Enough for a reviewer to judge provenance, not a full log.\",\"next\":\"done\",\"feedback\":\"Yes, bounded and checkable.\"}]},\"done\":{\"prompt\":\"Final disclosure: scoped decision, concrete contribution, accept/reject calls with reasons, verification, and an explicit statement that you remain responsible. It makes the work easier to scrutinize.\",\"feedback\":\"You replaced a shield with a record.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading treats disclosure as a confession to be minimized: say \"AI was used\" and move on. Minimal disclosure protects no one, because it gives a reviewer nothing to evaluate. The second misreading treats disclosure as a credential: cite the AI to make the work sound more rigorous (\"validated by AI,\" \"objective\"). That inverts the purpose; disclosure is for scrutiny, not endorsement, and accountability-shedding language is a red flag a reviewer is trained to catch. Honest disclosure is neither apologetic nor self-promoting; it is a precise record of what the tool did and what you decided.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the standard to AI assistance with a literature summary. A first-draft disclosure says: \"AI summarized the sources.\" Walk the checks. Scope: name which sources and for what purpose (the AI drafted one-sentence summaries of five readings to seed the related-work section). Contribution: state what it produced (five candidate summaries). Calls: record that you verified each summary against the original source, corrected two that misstated a finding, and discarded one for a source the AI could not actually access (a hallucination you caught). Accountability: state that you confirmed every cited claim against the primary text and are responsible for the related-work section. The revised disclosure does the real work: it tells a reviewer exactly where the AI touched the manuscript, surfaces that you caught a fabrication, and leaves no doubt about who stands behind the citations.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead a disclosure as a skeptical reviewer asking \"what can I actually check here?\" A strong reader spots the empty disclosure (no scope, no verification) and the dangerous one (accountability handed to the tool) and can rewrite either into a bounded, checkable statement. The tell of a good disclosure is that it makes the work *easier to scrutinize*, not harder: it points the reviewer to the exact decisions where AI was involved and the exact checks the human performed. A disclosure that makes you trust the work less, but understand it more, is doing its job.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe disclosure excerpt is strong when it scopes the AI contribution to a bounded decision, records the accept/reject calls with reasons, names the verification, and keeps accountability with the author, all without padding it into a transcript. The README should make it possible for a reviewer to locate, for at least one decision, exactly what the AI produced and how it was checked. Persuasive polish is beside the point here; the load-bearing quality is honesty a reviewer can verify.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry bounded disclosure into a high-stakes venue with a formal AI policy, such as a journal that requires authors to specify model, prompts, and the role AI played. The transfer is to scale the same four elements without changing their logic: scope becomes a per-section accounting, verification becomes a documented procedure, and accountability becomes an explicit author statement that AI is not credited as an author and that the humans are responsible. The discipline transfers because the ethic is constant, AI is a tool whose contributions you scope, check, and own, while the formality of the venue only changes how much detail that ethic must be expressed in.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design\", \"ai_use_disclosure\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/ail606-supplement/07-07-ethical-reporting-of-ai-use-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision\" course=\"AIL 606\" section=\"07.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the AI-USE disclosure excerpt tied to one bounded design-support decision for AIL 606 07.07. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to [cognitive load theory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cognitive_load). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"ail606_supplement_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the AI-USE disclosure excerpt records the bounded scope (which one design decision the AI touched), the concrete AI contribution, the accept/reject/rewrite calls with their reasons, the verification step you ran on the output, and an explicit statement that accountability stays with you, with no objectivity or credentialing language padding it out.\n\n## References\n\n- [Mayer multimedia learning](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781139164603)\n- Sweller, Ayres, and Kalyuga (2011). Cognitive Load Theory. Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4419-8126-4\n- UDL learner variability. https://udlguidelines.cast.org/\n- usability heuristics. https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"Which AI-use disclosure is specific enough to be honest and useful in a design report?","options":["AI was used in this project.","I used a generative model to draft three alternative item stems for screen 4; I revised two and discarded one, and verified all wording against the rubric myself.","No AI was used, though it actually was.","AI helped a little here and there."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An ethical disclosure names what the AI did, where, and how the human verified it. Vague or false statements defeat the purpose of disclosure.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_vague_blanket_disclosure","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"A disclosure says: \"All analysis was AI-generated and is therefore objective.\" What is the reporting flaw?","options":["There is no flaw; AI output is objective.","It claims authority for the AI and removes human accountability, when the designer is still responsible for verifying and standing behind the analysis.","It is too detailed.","It should not mention AI at all."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Disclosure must preserve human accountability. Framing AI output as inherently objective launders responsibility the designer cannot give away.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_ai_output_as_objective","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ail606_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"You want a disclosure tied to one bounded design-support decision. Which scope is right?","options":["Disclose nothing, since the work is mostly yours.","Disclose the specific decision the AI supported, the prompt intent, and your accept or reject calls, rather than a blanket statement covering the whole project.","Disclose every keystroke and the entire model configuration regardless of relevance.","Disclose only the parts that make you look good."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Bounded disclosure ties the report to the actual decision the AI touched and your judgment on it. That is more honest than silence, a blanket claim, or selective omission.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_disclosure_optional","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_use_disclosure","stem":"A teammate argues, \"We polished the wording afterward, so we do not need to mention the AI draft.\" Why is that reasoning wrong?","options":["They are right; editing the output erases the need to disclose.","Editing does not remove the AI's contribution to the underlying content, so the disclosure still owes an honest account of what the AI produced and how it was checked.","Disclosure is only needed if the AI wrote the final words verbatim.","Disclosure is a formality with no ethical weight."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Provenance follows the contribution, not the surface wording. Human editing does not retroactively make the work AI-free or excuse omission.","misconception_id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_disclosure_optional","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["ail606_supplement_research_translation_for_learning_design","ai_use_disclosure","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_vague_blanket_disclosure","pattern":"blanket_disclosure_without_specifics","description":"Reporting AI use with a vague blanket statement that names neither what the AI did nor how the human verified it","trigger":"disclosure says only that AI was used somewhere, with no decision, location, or verification","feedback":"Name what the AI produced, where, and how you checked it. A blanket line is not an honest account.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_ai_output_as_objective","pattern":"ai_output_framed_as_objective","description":"Framing AI-generated content as objective or authoritative to shift accountability away from the designer","trigger":"disclosure presents AI output as inherently neutral, so the human is no longer answerable for it","feedback":"Keep accountability with the designer. AI output is a draft you verified, not an objective authority.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"ail606_supplement_7_7_ethical_reporting_of_ai_use_disclosure_optional","pattern":"disclosure_treated_as_optional","description":"Assuming disclosure can be skipped if the work is mostly human, or if the AI draft was later edited","trigger":"learner omits disclosure because they polished the wording or judge their contribution as dominant","feedback":"Provenance follows the contribution, not the final wording. Disclose the bounded decision the AI supported even after you edit it.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"0f87318f8638fb82795cae45b8e14772c6497d6518b28b680a712caa0ebbe587","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}